cells:
- kind: 1
  value: '# Transactions & Concurrency — configuration and readiness


    Run as an **administrative user in the target PDB**, not SYS unless that is the supported connection. The configuration
    is intentionally separate from the application-schema setup in Session 1.


    **Scope:** Inspect priority settings; capture the original values; optionally configure short priority wait
    targets. The `ALTER SYSTEM` statements affect other workloads in this PDB and may be rejected on Autonomous
    Database Free. If unavailable, skip the Priority Transactions execution and use the remaining demonstrations.


    **Important:** Capture the original parameter values before changing anything. Run `transactions_concurrency_admin_cleanup.sql`
    afterward to restore the original values. Do not assume Oracle defaults match the prior environment.

    '
  languageId: markdown
- kind: 2
  value: "SELECT name, value, isdefault, issys_modifiable\nFROM v$parameter\nWHERE name IN ('priority_txns_mode',\
    \ 'priority_txns_high_wait_target',\n               'priority_txns_medium_wait_target', 'txn_priority')\nORDER\
    \ BY name;\n"
  languageId: oracle-sql
- kind: 1
  value: '## Administrative change — execute only on an isolated demo database


    **Why this matters:** A high-priority transaction can request a lock held by lower-priority work. In `TRACK`
    mode Oracle records qualifying conflicts; in `ROLLBACK` mode it can roll back the lower-priority blocker. A
    short wait target makes the effect visible, but changing system settings on a shared customer database is not
    appropriate without authorization.


    If the target PDB supports these commands, execute the three statements below. If you receive an insufficient
    privilege or unsupported-operation error on ADB Free, **do not retry as SYS**. Continue with Lock-Free Reservations
    and Sessionless Transactions and discuss Priority Transactions conceptually.

    '
  languageId: markdown
- kind: 2
  value: 'ALTER SYSTEM SET priority_txns_high_wait_target = 5;

    ALTER SYSTEM SET priority_txns_medium_wait_target = 5;

    ALTER SYSTEM SET priority_txns_mode = ROLLBACK;

    '
  languageId: oracle-sql
- kind: 1
  value: '## Confirm the effective configuration


    For the live priority test, `PRIORITY_TXNS_MODE` must be `ROLLBACK`, with a short `PRIORITY_TXNS_HIGH_WAIT_TARGET`.
    A session can set `TXN_PRIORITY` with `ALTER SESSION` **before** starting a transaction. If the system parameters
    cannot be modified, the priority section is an awareness discussion only.

    '
  languageId: markdown
- kind: 2
  value: "SELECT name, value FROM v$parameter\nWHERE name IN ('priority_txns_mode', 'priority_txns_high_wait_target',\n\
    \               'priority_txns_medium_wait_target')\nORDER BY name;\n"
  languageId: oracle-sql
- kind: 1
  value: '## References

    - https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/26/admin/managing-transactions.html

    - https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/26/refrn/PRIORITY_TXNS_MODE.html

    '
  languageId: markdown
